.TH FILECRYPT 1 "November 2026" "Operating Systems I" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
filecrypt \- encrypt and decrypt files using AES-256-GCM
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B filecrypt
.B -e
.B -p
.I password
.I input
.I output

.B filecrypt
.B -d
.B -p
.I password
.I input
.I output

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B filecrypt
encrypts and decrypts regular files using AES-256-GCM.
The encryption key is derived from the user's password
using PBKDF2 with SHA-256.

The program uses mmap() to map the input file into memory.
It uses sigaction() to handle interruption signals and
OPENSSL_cleanse() to clear sensitive data from memory.

Encrypted files contain a file header, random salt,
initialization vector (IV), ciphertext, and authentication tag.
The authentication tag is used to check whether the encrypted
file has been changed.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -e
Encrypt the input file.

.TP
.B -d
Decrypt the input file.

.TP
.B -p password
Specify the password used to derive the encryption key.

.TP
.B -h
Display usage information.

.SH EXAMPLES
Encrypt a file:
.PP
.B filecrypt -e -p mypassword input.txt encrypted.bin

.PP
Decrypt a file:
.PP
.B filecrypt -d -p mypassword encrypted.bin output.txt

.SH LIMITATIONS
The output file must not already exist.
Input and output paths must be different.
Passwords supplied through -p may be visible in process arguments.
Output files are created with permission 0600.

.SH ERROR HANDLING
The program rejects incorrect passwords, corrupted or
tampered files, and invalid file formats.
It also handles interruption signals such as Ctrl + C
(SIGINT) and SIGTERM.
Incomplete output files are removed when an operation fails
or is interrupted.

.SH SECURITY
AES-256-GCM provides encryption and authentication.
PBKDF2-SHA256 derives the encryption key using 200,000 iterations.
Random salts and IVs are generated for each encrypted file.
Sensitive memory is cleared using OPENSSL_cleanse().

.SH SEE ALSO
openssl(1), mmap(2), munmap(2), sigaction(2), getopt(3)